    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Hydrocarbon transport in fractures and faults at Orcun Oil Field, Santa Maria, California are analogous to processes operating at depth in the fractured Monterey Formation. We examined two sites where faulting juxtapose star-filled sand of the Pliocene Cebada Member of the Careaga Sand\r\nagainst relatively hydrocarbon-free siliceous shales and clay stones of the Sisquoc Formation. Hydrocarbons are found preferentially in long, continuous fractures which are sub-parallel to local reverse faulting, and to regional anticline axes. The distribution of hydrocarbon in fractures of the Sisquoc Fm. points to an association of hydrocarbon migration with fracturing and faulting in the low permeability rocks.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
